{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zksdk7qi2vzolznoelrwfzb456p4rnsm7ozqye564qssxyk3xuda","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result\n\n**The Rust translation is byte-for-byte equivalent to the C. I found no divergence, so `src/lib.rs` needed no changes.** The only change to `Cargo.toml` was adding `libloading` as a dev-dependency (fetched from the local cargo cache, since crates.io is blocked here).\n\n### Completion gate\n\n- [x] **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly `driver` and `run`; both present in the Rust `.so`. `comm -23` diff **empty**; 0 undefined non-libc symbols. `CMakeLists.txt` builds exactly one TU (`src/driver.c`), so no module could have been skipped — nothing had to be translated or stubbed.\n- [x] **Phase B** — all **28/28** `CONFIGS.md` rows pass across randomized inputs (fixed seed, splitmix64).\n- [x] **Phase C** — all **18/18** `ERRORS.md` rows have passing differential tests.\n- [x] **Every feature combination** — `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` table, so the combination set is `default` + `--no-default-features`; both pass in **debug and release** (the release `.so` is differentially tested via `RUST_DRIVER_SO`).\n\n### What made this worth doing\n\nSymbols matched and the happy path passed within the first few minutes. That was not sufficient. Two things mattered more:\n\n**A harness bug that looked like a real divergence.** My first Phase B run showed 5 \"failures\" where the Rust output had `\"ok\"` or `\"test row17 ... \"` spliced into it. That was libtest's own progress text landing in my fd-1 capture from parallel threads — not a translation bug. Fixed by pinning `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` (with an assertion in the harness so it can't silently regress) rather than by \"fixing\" the Rust.\n\n**Mutation testing found two genuine blind spots.** I injected 25 deliberate bugs; the first suite version let two survive:\n\n1. **`bathrooms` narrowed to `f32` was invisible.** `bathrooms` is only ever `k + 0.5`, which `f32` represents *exactly* below 2²³ — so no shallow test could ever see the narrowing. I added a test driving `bathrooms` past 8,388,608 (~40 s) and stepping across the limit one call at a time.\n2. **Three classic C→Rust errors were unreachable** by per-row output comparison, and now have dedicated tests: `thread_local!` instead of `static mut` for the global `the_house` (caught by calling from a second thread), `printf` reimplemented in Rust (caught only under `LC_NUMERIC=de_DE.utf8`, where libc prints `,5`), and writing through the cast-away `const char *` (caught with an `mprotect(PROT_READ)` page).\n\nFinal tally: **24/24 killable mutants caught, 0 missed.**\n\n### One thing I want to flag explicitly\n\nThe single surviving mutant — deleting `parse_val`'s `errno == 0` check — is **not** a test gap. I didn't want to just assert that, so I proved it: glibc's `strtol` sets `ERANGE` only on overflow, when it returns `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, and on LP64 both already fail the `int` range check. A probe over every digit-run of length 1–500 (all 10 leading digits, both signs), all `long`/`int` boundary literals, and 3,000,000 random byte strings found **0** inputs where `errno != 0` while `tmp` is in `int` range. So no differential test can ever distinguish it. It's recorded in `mutation_check.py`'s `EQUIVALENT` set with that reasoning. The `errno` *reset* is a different matter and is genuinely load-bearing — removing it is caught.\n\nArtifacts in `translation/`: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `VERIFICATION.md`, `verify.sh` (one-command reproduction), `mutation_check.py`, and 50 tests across 4 files. `c_src/` sources are untouched (md5s verified); only the instructed `c_src/build/` was added."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":883,"cache_read_input_tokens":154635,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":883,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e967bdb-9af5-42a7-86c3-9f92c2ba4c55","uuid":"ab170636-d9ab-4b65-9a62-b974564164d1","timestamp":"2026-08-28T14:50:08.418Z"}
